[561.789718, "o", "\r\n## How the trick works\r\n\r\n```\r\n#if 0            ← Python: an ordinary comment. gcc: block skipped.\r\n  ...Python...   ← gcc never parses it.\r\n_polyglot_c_source = r\"\"\"\"    ← opens a raw string with 4 quotes\r\n#endif\r\n  ...C...        ← Python: it is just text inside that string.\r\n/* \"\"\"  # this closes the raw string\r\n```\r\n\r\nTwo details that took measurement rather than assumption:\r\n\r\n- **gcc lexes skipped `#if 0` bodies line by line**, and warns (`missing terminating \" character`) on any line with an odd number of quotes — even at `-w`-off defaults. So every Python line must be quote-balanced. That ruled out docstrings (`\"\"\"` alone on a line) and measured quoting, so the Python half uses `#` comments exclusively.\r\n- **`r\"\"\"\"`**: Python takes the first three quotes as the delimiter and the fourth as content, so the string opens with one trailing quote — which is what balances that line for gcc. The C half then contains free `\"` and `'` (it has apostrophes in comments) with no escaping, because Python is inside a *raw* string.\r\n\r\nNeither side ever parses the other's code, so both halves are plain idiomatic code rather than deliberately obfuscated lines.\r\n\r\n## Correctness\r\n\r\nBoth halves use fast doubling (O(log n)). The C half computes in `uint64_t` up to f(93) = 12200160415121876738, then switches to a base-1e9 bignum, so it is exact for *all* N, not just those fitting in 64 bits — matching Python, which is why f(20000) and f(100000) (20899 digits) agree byte-for-byte.\r\n\r\nTwo real bugs found and fixed by testing, not inspection:\r\n1. `fib_u64` shifted a **32-bit `unsigned`** by up to 63 — undefined behaviour; x86 masked the count to `n >> 0`, fabricating a set bit at i=32 and returning garbage for n=1. Fixed by shifting a 64-bit copy.\r\n2. Python 3.12 refuses int→str above 4300 digits (`ValueError: Exceeds the limit`), which crashed `python3 main.py.c 20000` while the C side worked. Fixed with a guarded `sys.set_int_max_str_digits(0)`.\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\n`verify_polyglot.sh` (left in the directory) asserts every constraint and **exits 0 — ALL CHECKS PASSED**:\r\n\r\n- both exact commands from your request, run from a neutral cwd → `832040`\r\n- **zero warnings** from plain `gcc`, from `gcc -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 -pedantic`, and `py_compile`; also clean under `-fsanitize=address,undefined` up to f(100000), no leaks\r\n- both binaries vs an independent O(n) reference at k = 0–5, 10, 20, 40, **91, 92, 93, 94, 95** (the `uint64_t`/bignum seam), 200, 1000, 5000\r\n- cross-mode agreement at 20000 and 100000\r\n- bad input (`\"\"`, `abc`, `-1`, `3x`) exits 2 in both with a one-line message and no traceback\r\n\r\nOn the file name: you specified the path, so `main.py.c` it is. Note that `python3 main.py.c` and `gcc main.py.c` both work, but a bare `python3 main.py` won't — the `.c` is part of the name.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=34 surface=a57c8a7444d5\r\n"]
